  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nOur approach is to model the problem as a constrained non-linear optimization task. The aim is to find a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C\u2085, subject to h being within [0,1] and having an integral of 1 over [0,2]. We employ a discretization strategy and use CVXPY for optimization due to its support for complex constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\n- Define h as a vector of n_points variables, each in [0,1].\n- Integral constraint: sum(h) * dx = 1.\n- For each lag k, compute the correlation with 1-h:\n  correlation(k) = sum(h[i] * (1 - h[i + k - n_points]) for valid i)\n- Objective: minimize max(correlation(k) * dx) over all k.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Discretization:\n   - Choose n_points (initially 70).\n   - Compute dx = 2.0 / n_points.\n\nb. Correlation constraints:\n   - For each lag k in [0, 2*n_points - 1]:\n      correlation_k = sum(h[i] * (1 - h_shifted[i]) for i in valid range)\n      add constraint: correlation_k <= C\n\nc. Optimization:\n   - Use CVXPY with MOSEK or SCS solver.\n   - Variables: h and C.\n   - Minimize C.\n   - Apply constraints: 0 <= h[i] <= 1, sum(h) = n_points / 2, and all correlation_k <= C.\n\nd. Termination:\n   - Stop when time budget exhausted or C converges.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- n_points: 70 is a good start; try 100 if feasible.\n- Initial h: use initial_h_values or random with sum constraint.\n- Lags: consider all lags for thoroughness.\n- Error tolerance: adaptive based on solver feedback.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n- CVXPY with MOSEK is efficient for this size.\n- Expect to solve within 1100s with n_points=70.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n- Ensure h is within [0,1] and sum constraint holds.\n- Verify C is the smallest possible.\n- Compute final C using actual cross-correlations.\n- Return best h and C found.",
